    <h1>Selling an Inherited Home in Los Angeles: Trust Sales, Probate, Taxes, and Next Steps</h1>
    <div class="lede-line"></div>
    <div class="body">

      <p>Losing someone is hard enough without a house to deal with. If you've inherited a home in Los Angeles, whether as an heir, a successor trustee, or an executor, take a breath. Almost nothing about the house has to be decided this week. This guide explains how the sale works, what to sort out first, and where the tax questions come in, in plain English.</p>

      <h2>First, how was the home held?</h2>
      <p>Everything else depends on this. A home usually passes in one of three ways: through a <strong>living trust</strong>, through <strong>probate</strong>, or through a simpler transfer such as a beneficiary (transfer-on-death) deed. Your attorney, or the trust documents themselves, will tell you which one applies.</p>

      <h2>Trust sale vs. probate sale</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Trust sale.</strong> When the home is in a living trust, the successor trustee reviews the trust and the trustee's powers. If the authority is clear, the sale generally moves more directly than a probate sale and without court supervision.</li>
        <li><strong>Probate sale.</strong> When there's no trust, the court appoints an executor (or an administrator, if there's no will or named executor), and that person needs legal authority before selling. When there's no will, the administrator's authority is often more limited, with court approval needed for certain actions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The question that decides probate timing: IAEA authority</h3>
      <p>In probate, the court can give the executor <strong>full or limited authority</strong> under the Independent Administration of Estates Act (IAEA).</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Full authority:</strong> the sale works much like a normal home sale, with a short notice period to the heirs and no court hearing.</li>
        <li><strong>Limited or no authority:</strong> the sale needs <strong>court confirmation</strong>. That means a petition, public notice, a hearing, a minimum price (generally 90% of the appraised value), and the possibility that other buyers bid higher in court.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Ask your probate attorney early which one applies. It affects how long the sale takes and how certain the closing is.</p>

      <h2>Could the home skip probate altogether?</h2>
      <p>Sometimes. For deaths on or after April 1, 2025, a decedent's <strong>primary residence valued at up to $750,000</strong> can transfer through a simplified court petition instead of full probate. Many Los Angeles homes are worth more than that, so most local families end up with either a trust sale or a full probate.</p>

      <h2>Taxes: the part most people worry about, and the good news</h2>
      <h3>Federal capital gains: the stepped-up basis</h3>
      <p>When you inherit a home, its tax basis generally resets to the home's <strong>fair market value on the date of death</strong>, not what the original owner paid decades ago. You owe capital gains tax only on the growth after that date.</p>
      <p><em>Hypothetical example:</em> a home worth $1,800,000 on the date of death that sells for $1,830,000 produces roughly $30,000 of taxable gain, not the gain from the 1980s purchase price. Because that date-of-death value matters, it's smart to get a <strong>date-of-death appraisal</strong> and keep it with your records.</p>

      <h3>Property tax and Prop 19: mostly a keep-or-sell question</h3>
      <p>If you plan to <strong>sell</strong>, the buyer is assessed at the new price anyway, so Prop 19 matters mainly if you're thinking about <em>keeping</em> the home. In that case, a child can keep the parent's low property tax base only if:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the home was the parent's principal residence,</li>
        <li>the child moves in and makes it their primary residence within one year, and files for the homeowners' exemption within that year, and</li>
        <li>the home's value stays within a limit: the parent's taxable value plus $1 million (the figure is adjusted every other year).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Anything above the limit is added to the tax base, and second homes and rentals don't qualify. The claim is filed with the county assessor on Form BOE-19-P. See our <a href="/prop-19/">Prop 19 guide</a> for the downsizing side of the rules.</p>

      <h2>How we help you sell</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Confirm who has authority</strong> to sell, working alongside your attorney or trustee counsel.</li>
        <li><strong>Get the numbers:</strong> a market valuation and a date-of-death value for your CPA, so you can decide whether to keep or sell.</li>
        <li><strong>Handle the house:</strong> sorting, donation, estate-sale and clean-out teams, light prep or staging if it makes sense. You are never required to renovate, and we'll tell you honestly whether updates would pay off or whether an as-is sale nets you the most.</li>
        <li><strong>Market it your way:</strong> on the open market, or quietly and off-market.</li>
        <li><strong>Close:</strong> including the court confirmation step if your probate requires it.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If you're working with a <a href="/blog/selling-a-house-in-probate-california/">probate realtor in Los Angeles</a>, they should be able to explain all of this clearly and without pressure. That's the standard we hold ourselves to.</p>

      <h2>Questions heirs and trustees ask us</h2>
      <h3>Can the house be sold while probate is still open?</h3>
      <p>Yes, once the executor has legal authority. Depending on the level of IAEA authority, the sale may also need court confirmation.</p>
      <h3>Do we have to clean out and fix up the house first?</h3>
      <p>No. Many inherited homes sell as-is. We help you decide whether a few targeted updates would return more than they cost.</p>
      <h3>Do all the heirs have to agree?</h3>
      <p>It depends on the trust or court order. With several heirs or co-trustees, decisions are usually made together as the documents provide, and your attorney can guide that conversation.</p>
      <h3>Will I owe a lot of capital gains tax?</h3>
      <p>Usually much less than people fear, because of the stepped-up basis. The exact number depends on the date-of-death value and your sale price, so confirm it with your CPA.</p>
      <h3>How long does it take?</h3>
      <p>A trust sale can move fairly quickly. Probate depends on the authority the court grants and the court's calendar, so the earlier you start the paperwork, the better.</p>

      <p class="disclaimer">This page is general information, not legal or tax advice. Probate and trust rules depend on your documents and your court, so talk with a California probate or trust attorney and a CPA about your situation.</p>
